---
//Methodology page — the exact definitions behind every stat on the site.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const LAST_UPDATED = '2026-08-29';
const origin = (Astro.site ?? new URL('https://ranklock.app')).origin;
const jsonLd = breadcrumbJsonLd(origin, [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Methodology', path: '/methodology' },
]);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology — how RankLock computes its stats | RankLock"
  description="How RankLock computes its Deadlock stats: data source, rank bands, win-rate and pick-rate formulas, patch attribution, refresh cadence."
  canonicalPath="/methodology"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <main class="container" style="padding-top:40px; padding-bottom:64px;">
    <div class="legal">
      <div class="kicker" style="margin-bottom:10px;">Last updated · {LAST_UPDATED}</div>
      <h1 class="h-hero" style="font-size:clamp(32px,5vw,48px); margin-bottom:14px;">How RankLock computes its stats</h1>
      <p style="font-size:16px; color:var(--text-2); max-width:72ch; margin-bottom:8px;">
        Every number on RankLock is computed from public match data by our own
        pipeline. This page records the definitions behind those numbers: what the
        data source is, what a rank means, how win-rates and pick-rates are
        calculated, how matches are assigned to patches, how often the numbers
        refresh, and what the site deliberately does not publish.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
      <p>
        RankLock reads match data published by the public
        <a href="https://deadlock-api.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">deadlock-api</a>
        project. Every aggregated stat on the site is computed over the 6v6
        competitive queue. The upstream data stores that queue as
        <code>match_mode = 'Unranked'</code>; no separate <code>'Ranked'</code> value
        exists, so &ldquo;competitive&rdquo; on RankLock means exactly that filter,
        applied in every aggregation job.
      </p>
      <p>
        Matches are loaded in bulk and stored, and statistics are computed over
        windows of that stored history. What the website serves comes from
        precomputed tables, assembled by a separate analytics pipeline.
      </p>

      <h2>What a rank means on RankLock</h2>
      <p>
        No stat on RankLock is computed from raw MMR. The pipeline does not read a
        player's MMR number at all. What it reads is a <strong>badge</strong>: a
        team-average rank value that the upstream match data assigns to every
        player in the match. All six players on a team carry the same badge for
        that match.
      </p>
      <p>The badge drives three groupings, depending on the page:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A 12-band ladder</strong> — band = badge &divide; 10, giving bands
          0 to 11. This powers the rank selector on hero pages.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Five brackets</strong> — badge ranges 11&ndash;36, 41&ndash;56,
          61&ndash;76, 81&ndash;96 and 101&ndash;116, plus an &ldquo;all
          ranks&rdquo; view. The API labels them with the game's rank names, from
          Initiate&ndash;Seeker at the bottom to Ascendant&ndash;Eternus at the
          top. Matchup stats use this grouping.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A 4-way bracket</strong> — low (below 80), mid (below 100), high
          (below 116) and top (116 and up). Hero pages offer this as a simpler
          filter.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A player's own badge is the badge of their latest match. The leaderboard
        keeps one row per account and game mode, uses the player's latest non-null
        match badge, requires at least five matches, and sorts by badge, highest
        first.
      </p>

      <h2>How win-rates and pick-rates are calculated</h2>
      <p>
        A win-rate is the share of player-slots that won. When a hero shows a
        54.2% win-rate, that hero won 54.2% of the player-slots it appeared in
        within the group you are looking at, rounded to one decimal. A 6v6 match
        produces twelve player-slots, and picks are counted the same way: a
        hero's pick count is the number of player-slots it occupied.
      </p>
      <p>
        On patch pages, pick-rate divides a hero's appearances by the number of
        distinct matches in the group. Both teams can pick the same hero in one
        match, and each pick counts, so the pick-rate scale runs from 0 to 2.
      </p>
      <p>
        Patch win-rates come with a guardrail: when a hero has fewer than 20
        appearances in a group, the API returns no value instead of a percentage.
        Small samples produce meaningless swings, so they are suppressed. The
        &ldquo;movers&rdquo; listed on a patch page are the heroes whose win-rate
        moved most compared with the immediately previous patch by release date.
      </p>
      <p>
        Matchup pages answer a narrower question: when hero A faces hero B, how
        often does A win? The win-rate there is A's wins divided by the matches
        where both heroes appeared, computed per game mode. Filtering that view
        by bracket constrains side A's badge only.
      </p>

      <h2>How a match is assigned to a patch</h2>
      <p>
        A match belongs to the patch that was live when the match started: its
        start time falls inside the patch's release-to-end window. One patch is
        open at any moment — its end date is not yet set — and it covers matches
        up to the present. Once a match is attributed, it never moves to another
        patch retroactively.
      </p>
      <p>
        The job that refreshes the patch registry from the upstream changelog is
        not part of the source we publish from, so we describe only its observed
        behavior: a patch's release date and label never change once set, and only
        its end date is updated when the next patch arrives.
      </p>

      <h2>Lane Lab and player stats</h2>
      <p>
        Lane Lab economy curves show the 25th, 50th and 75th percentile of net
        worth per time bin for a rank band. The pipeline stores only counts in
        fixed buckets — three-minute time bins and 1,000 net-worth steps for Lane
        Lab, one-minute bins and 500 steps on the stats surface — and the
        percentile is reconstructed from those counts when you ask for it. No
        percentile is stored.
      </p>
      <p>
        The early-economy verdict takes one net-worth reading per player per
        match, the reading nearest the 9-minute mark between 9:00 and 10:00, and
        reports the win-rate of players at each reading level within a band.
        Player percentiles average a player's per-match ratios — kills plus
        assists per death, with deaths floored at one — and rank that average
        against every other player in the same bracket and game mode. The
        &ldquo;improve&rdquo; benchmarks compare a player with averages and
        nearest-rank percentiles per bracket, hero and match-duration bucket,
        computed from normal-mode matches only.
      </p>

      <h2>When the numbers refresh</h2>
      <p>
        There is no fixed daily or weekly recomputation. Each pipeline run takes
        the window from the last completed run, recorded in a ledger, to the
        current hour. It computes that window's contribution, folds it exactly
        once into long-lived accumulators, and rebuilds the served tables from
        them. The schedule behind this is a six-hour timer that is currently
        switched on by hand, so stats refresh in bursts when the pipeline runs —
        not on a guaranteed six-hour tick. When the timer will run unattended is
        an operator decision, not something the code decides.
      </p>
      <p>
        You never have to guess how old a number is. Every analytics response
        carries an <code>X-Data-Fresh-As-Of</code> header with the timestamp of
        the last successful run, and data past its freshness threshold — twelve
        hours for matchup stats, 48 hours for daily and lane datasets — is flagged
        with <code>X-Data-Stale: true</code> instead of being passed off as fresh.
        A route whose dataset has never been computed answers
        &ldquo;computing&rdquo; rather than inventing a number.
      </p>

      <h2>What RankLock does not publish</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Raw MMR.</strong> No MMR number is stored or served anywhere;
          only the rounded badge exists. The per-day MMR history endpoint still
          answers, but it is empty for most accounts because the upstream MMR
          feed no longer delivers data. Player pages show the last 1,000 badged
          matches instead.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rank distribution.</strong> The endpoint is live but returns an
          empty set today: the badge field it counts has not been backfilled
          across all players yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Per-rank item win-rates.</strong> Item win-rates are computed
          for all ranks only. The per-rank read exists but returns empty rather
          than a fabricated number.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Item tiers, counters and synergies computed by RankLock.</strong>
          Item stats by bracket, hero counters and hero synergies are proxied from
          deadlock-api's analytics service and are labeled by that origin in the
          API. The all-ranks item win-rates are the one item dataset RankLock
          computes itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Arbitrary per-player match timelines.</strong> The detail tier
          is never scanned in bulk to answer a request. The single carve-out is a
          player's own economy curve in Lane Lab.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cohort overlay against your own rank.</strong> Comparing your
          economy curve with your own rank band requires a service that is built
          but not deployed, so those routes are not served.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to check a number yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Three public endpoints show the state behind any stat. The
        <a href="https://api.ranklock.app/api/meta/data-horizon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">data horizon</a>
        reports the newest match in the database and the coverage window of every
        dataset. The freshness headers travel with every analytics response. And
        <a href="https://api.ranklock.app/api/patches/current" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the current patch</a>
        shows the open window that every patch stat is attributed to. If a number
        on the site ever disagrees with what those endpoints imply, treat the
        endpoints as the truth and
        <a href="/contact">tell us</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
